# Clean Code

Source: Robert C. Martin, _Clean Code_. Inventory: `docs/clean-code-inventory.md`. Angular file/class/selector conventions stay under PB-87 — do not enforce them here.

## Meaningful Names (Ch.2)

### Use Intention-Revealing Names

Prefer names that say why the thing exists and how it is used (`amountCents`, `deletedAt`, `sortOrder`) over opaque abbreviations or dumping-ground locals (`data`, `result` with no context).

### Avoid Disinformation

Do not name something like a type or structure it is not (`*List` that is not a list, `*Manager` that does not manage). Avoid false cues and near-homophones.

### Make Meaningful Distinctions

Distinctions must carry meaning — avoid noise suffixes (`Info`, `Data`, `Object`) and numbered series (`a1`, `a2`). One clear name per concept.

### Use Pronounceable Names

Prefer spoken words over packed encodings (`generationTimestamp`, not `genymdhms`-style names).

### Use Searchable Names / Avoid Mental Mapping

Avoid single-letter names except classic loop indices (`i`/`j`/`k`) or unused `_`. Readers should not translate `c`→category in their head. Prefer greppable names over magic numbers.

### Avoid Encodings

No Hungarian / type prefixes (`ICategory`, `strName`, `m_`). Member encodings discouraged. Casing is gated by ESLint (`naming-convention`, `id-length`, `id-denylist`).

### Class Names / Method Names

Classes: nouns or noun phrases (`CategoryService`, `BudgetCell`, `PeriodBalance`). Methods: verbs or verb phrases (`listByType`, `softDelete`, `updateAmount`). Clear accessors (`getById`).

### Don't Be Cute

No jokey or culture-bound names. Prefer clear over clever.

### Pick One Word per Concept / Don't Pun

Prefer one verb per idea. Store API: `list` / `getById` / `insert` / `update` (soft-delete via store semantics) — do not mix `fetch` / `retrieve` / `obtain` for the same operation. Do not reuse one word for two meanings (e.g. `add` for both insert and arithmetic).

### Solution Domain vs Problem Domain

CS/Angular vocabulary is fine where it fits (`Router`, `signal`, `inject`, `schema`, queue, map). In feature/domain code prefer PB vocabulary:

- `Category`, `CategoryType` (`income` | `expense` | `savings`)
- `BudgetCell`, `Settings`
- Money amounts in **cents** (`amountCents`, `*Cents` totals)
- Soft-delete / `deletedAt`
- Plan year / month
- `PeriodBalance`, `PeriodTotals`
- `DOCUMENT_STORE`, `COLLECTIONS`

### Meaningful Context / Don't Add Gratuitous Context

Add enough context when bare names are ambiguous (`monthIncomeCents` vs bare `amount`). Prefer well-named classes/modules as context over long prefixes on every local. Avoid redundant stamps (`Category.categoryName` → `Category.name`; no `PB`/`App` on every identifier).

## Functions (Ch.3)

ESLint (prod TS): `complexity` 5, `max-depth` 2, `max-params` 3, `max-lines-per-function` 20 (skip blank/comments); `no-restricted-syntax` bans inline object types on parameters. Specs exclude the size/`max-*`/`complexity` rules (dual standard — see `.cursor/rules/unit-tests.mdc`).

### Small!

Keep functions short. Prefer extracting to `helpers/` or `src/data/lib/` over growing a method past the line gate.

### Do One Thing

If you can extract another meaningfully named function, the original was doing more than one thing. Each extract should be a single responsibility.

### One Level of Abstraction per Function

Keep one abstraction level per function. Orchestration in components/services; detail in helpers/`lib`. Nesting depth is gated by ESLint.

### Reading Code from Top to Bottom — The Stepdown Rule

Prefer a top-down narrative: public method → named helpers at the next level of detail.

### Switch Statements

Prefer polymorphism for large type switches; bury unavoidable small switches once. Broader smell gates are PB-104.

### Use Descriptive Names

Function names say what they do — enforced under Meaningful Names (Ch.2). Extracted helpers must get intention-revealing names.

### Function Arguments

- Prefer 0–2 args; at most 3 positional. More → one argument object with a **named** `type`/`interface` (never an inline `{ ... }` on the parameter).
- Common monadic forms: keep single-arg transforms/events clear (`dollarsToCents`, `cellKey`); do not wrap one value in an unrelated options bag.
- Flag arguments: avoid boolean params that mean two behaviors; split into two named functions or use an explicit union/enum field on a named options type.
- Dyadic functions: when two values are one concept, prefer a named argument object; property names act as keywords.
- Argument lists: rest/`...items` only for homogeneous lists of the same conceptual type — not mixed options.
- Verbs and keywords: method names are verb phrases (`save`, `listByType`, `attemptWhilePending`); named argument properties supply the keywords (`categoryId`, `failMessage`).

### Have No Side Effects

Do not change state beyond what the name promises (no hidden setup, caching, or UI writes in a check/transform). Commands (`save`, `softDelete`, `attemptWhilePending`) may mutate when that is the named purpose. Queries and pure helpers stay free of side effects.

### Output Arguments

Prefer return values over mutating out-params.

### Command Query Separation

Do not mix mutation with answering questions when that muddies intent. Keep `list`/`getById` separate from `save`/`insert`/`update`.

### Prefer Exceptions to Returning Error Codes

See Error Handling (Ch.7).

### Don't Repeat Yourself

Prefer helpers/`lib` extracts over copy-paste. Automated duplication detection is PB-104.

### Structured Programming

Keep nesting and branchiness low — gated by `max-depth` and `complexity`.

## Comments (Ch.4)

ESLint: `no-warning-comments` bans `FIXME` / `XXX` / `HACK` (location start). Temporary `TODO` comments are allowed when they state why (and a Jira key when one exists). Do not require JSDoc.

**Primary emphasis:** Don't Use a Comment When You Can Use a Function or a Variable — rename or extract until a narrative comment is unnecessary.

**Comment style:** No Jira keys (`PB-…`) in comments. Prefer complete sentences with full stops.

### Comments Do Not Make Up for Bad Code

Prefer fixing names, structure, or extracts over explaining a mess.

### Explain Yourself in Code

Intent lives in identifiers and small functions first. Drop “what” comments once the code already says it.

### Good Comments

Keep comments that naming cannot carry:

- **Legal** — copyright/license headers when needed
- **Informative** — rare non-obvious units, formats, or conventions
- **Explanation of Intent** — why this approach (e.g. parent owns persist; sparse cell ≠ zero)
- **Clarification** — awkward but necessary constructs (e.g. IndexedDB skips null keys, so soft-delete is filtered in memory)
- **Warning of Consequences** — surprising behavior (e.g. clear amounts on year change so stale cells do not show)
- **TODO** — temporary only; state why; prefer a Jira key. Do not use `FIXME` / `XXX` / `HACK`
- **Amplification** — emphasize something that looks insignificant but matters

Do not mandate JSDoc on public or private APIs (local Angular app, not a published library).

### Bad Comments

Avoid or delete:

- **Mumbling** — vague or unfinished; rewrite or remove
- **Redundant / Noise** — restates the identifier or next line (Zod “inferred type” boilerplate, empty-tab banners that repeat the class name, field docs that only echo the property name)
- **Misleading** — lies after refactors; update or delete with the code
- **Mandated / Function Headers / Nonpublic JSDoc** — no required comment on every function or private member
- **Journal / Attributions** — use git history, not change-log or “written by” lines in source (legal headers stay under Legal)
- **Position Markers / Closing Brace Comments** — no section dividers or `} // end if`
- **Commented-Out Code** — delete; recover from git
- **Nonlocal / Too Much Information / Inobvious Connection** — keep comments short, local, and clearly tied to the construct they describe

### Don't Use a Comment When You Can Use a Function or a Variable

If a comment exists to explain what a block or expression means, the fix is a better name or a small extract (`helpers/` / `src/data/lib/`) — not a better comment. Well-named helpers need no restating JSDoc; the function *is* the documentation. Keep true why / clarification / warning comments that naming cannot replace.

## Formatting (Ch.5)

Prettier is the horizontal and team formatting source of truth (`.prettierrc`, `npm run format`, `prettier --check` in `npm run lint`, lint-staged). Do not fight it with ESLint style rules — `eslint-config-prettier` stays last. Vertical narrative (newspaper, distance, ordering) is judgment under this chapter; Prettier preserves a single blank line and collapses doubles.

### The Purpose of Formatting

Formatting is communication. Prefer the team’s shared style over personal taste. Machine format comes from Prettier; readable vertical story comes from how we order and open the file.

### The Newspaper Metaphor

A source file reads like a newspaper article: headline and high-level story first, details afterward. Put the public or exported API and orchestration near the top; push helpers and lower-level detail down the file. Aligns with the Functions stepdown rule (Ch.3) at method level.

### Vertical Openness Between Concepts

Separate distinct concepts with a single blank line (imports from code; class members; guard vs work vs persist; fetch vs map vs set). Never use two consecutive blank lines — Prettier collapses them.

### Vertical Density

Keep tightly related lines packed with no blank between them (one short expression chain, one thought). Openness without density turns the file into sparse noise.

### Vertical Distance

Keep closely related concepts near each other. Prefer declaring a local near its first use. Avoid large gaps between a caller and the helper it depends on when affinity allows clustering them.

### Variable Declarations

Declare variables as close as practical to their first use. Do not hoist locals to the top of a long function when they belong with a later concept.

### Dependent Functions

Callees sit below their callers (stepdown) so dependencies point down. A helper with one caller sits directly under that caller. A helper shared by peer callers sits below the last of those callers (not under the first). File-level **functions** used by a class belong below the class, not above it, unless a language constraint requires otherwise. Module-level **constants and types** stay above the class.

### Conceptual Affinity

Group by concept: a public or protected operation next to its private helper pair is better than dumping every private at the bottom when that increases distance (e.g. `constructor` then `load`, `add` then `addCategory`). Similar concepts stay together; unrelated concepts do not interleave without reason. Shared helpers still follow Dependent Functions — keep the shared pair together below the last peer caller rather than inserting them under the first use.

### Vertical Ordering

Prefer a stable top-down story: module constants/types → fields and high-level API → concept clusters (caller then callee) → shared lower-level helpers. Visibility is not a hard sort key — do not pull a private helper away from its only caller just to keep every protected method above every private one. Within a concept cluster, caller above callee.

### Horizontal Openness and Density / Indentation / Dummy Scopes

Spaces around operators and after commas come from Prettier. Indentation is 2 spaces (`.editorconfig` + Prettier). Empty blocks still use braces and normal indentation — no cute one-liners that hide structure.

### Horizontal Alignment

Do not column-align declarations or assignments. Alignment creates noisy diffs when names change; Prettier does not align columns — leave it that way.

### Team Rules

One style for the whole team: shared `.prettierrc`, format on commit via lint-staged, check in CI through `npm run lint`. Angular template/component conventions beyond Prettier’s `parser: "angular"` stay under PB-87.

## Objects and Data Structures (Ch.6)

No new ESLint rules for this chapter. Member-chain / LoD gates false-positive on DTO field reads, Angular signals, and Dexie fluent APIs. Feature → Dexie / `createDocumentStore` import walls stay under Boundaries (PB-97) and `.cursor/rules/document-store.mdc`. Class SRP/size stays under PB-99. Spec DOM query style stays under `.cursor/rules/unit-tests.mdc`.

### Data Abstraction

Objects hide implementation behind verbs. Domain services (`CategoryService`, `BudgetCellService`, `SettingsService`), `DocumentStore`, and `PersistQueue` keep collaborators private (`store`, `database`, `chain`) and expose named operations (`list`, `getById`, `insert`, `update`, `softDelete`). Callers must not depend on how those objects persist or query.

Dexie fluent chains (`database.documents.where(...).equals(...).toArray()`) belong **inside** `DocumentStore` only — that is the hidden implementation. Do not reach Dexie from features; that gate is PB-97.

### Data/Object Anti-Symmetry

Objects hide data and expose functions. Data structures expose data and have no meaningful functions. Pick one shape per type.

PB choice: domain payloads are **data structures** (Zod schemas + `z.infer`, or plain `interface`s such as `PeriodBalance` / `PeriodTotals`). Behavior lives on injectables and pure helpers in `helpers/` and `src/data/lib/` — not methods on `Category` or `BudgetCell`. Do not push Active Record or rich entity APIs onto Zod DTOs.

### The Law of Demeter

A method should talk only to itself, its parameters, objects it creates, or its direct component fields — not to strangers reached through returned objects.

Prefer one hop to owned collaborators (`this.store.list`, `this.categories.revision()`). Avoid chaining across module boundaries into another type’s internals.

Reading fields on a returned DTO (`category.name`, `settings?.startingYear`, `cell.amountCents`) is **not** a LoD violation — that is using a data structure. Spec DOM trains (`fixture.nativeElement.querySelector...`) are test harness concern (`.cursor/rules/unit-tests.mdc`), not production LoD.

### Train Wrecks

Long chains that dig through foreign objects are a smell. Do not train-wreck through service or store façades from features. Fluent query APIs inside `DocumentStore` are accepted: the store owns `database`.

Accepted pragmatic dig (domain module only, not feature UI): Zod’s public `schema.shape.field` for single-field parse/reuse (e.g. `categorySchema.shape.name.safeParse`). Do not spread schema-shape digging into components. Angular `event.target` / `currentTarget` is a platform hop on a parameter you do not own — allowed.

### Hybrids

Half object / half data structure (public fields **and** significant behavior) is worse than either pure form. Do not grow business methods onto Zod DTOs or turn services into public field bags. Keep transforms in `helpers/` / `src/data/lib/`.

### Hiding Structure

When you have a data structure, do not wrap every field behind getters just to look object-oriented — that forces train wrecks without buying abstraction. Templates and features may read open DTO fields. Do not invent getter wrappers on `Category` / `BudgetCell` “for encapsulation.”

### Data Transfer Objects

DTOs are bags of public data with little or no behavior — the right shape for crossing boundaries. Treat Zod-inferred types and `StoreDocument<T>` / `PeriodTotals` / `PeriodBalance` as DTOs. Parse before persist at the service/store edge; map for the UI with pure helpers. Import and Dexie wrapping rules stay under document-store / PB-97.

### Active Record

Do not put navigation or persistence on the record type. `Category` / `BudgetCell` / `Settings` must not gain `save` / `delete` / relation walks. Persistence stays on `*Service` and `DOCUMENT_STORE`.

## Error Handling (Ch.7)

ESLint: `no-empty` (no empty `catch`); `no-throw-literal`; `prefer-promise-reject-errors`. Recommended also supplies `no-ex-assign`, `no-unsafe-finally`, `no-useless-catch`. Type-aware `@typescript-eslint/only-throw-error` and async/promise footguns stay under PB-76.

### Use Exceptions Rather Than Return Codes

Prefer `throw new Error(...)` for unexpected or invariant failures (e.g. category not found in a domain service). Do not invent error-code APIs (`{ ok: false }`, numeric codes, stringly failure statuses) that force every caller to check. UI work translates failures at the boundary with `attemptWhilePending` and a user-facing string — do not bubble Result types through components.

### Write Your Try-Catch-Finally Statement First

A function that catches should treat try/catch/finally as its one thing and leave state consistent. `runWhilePending` clears `PendingTasks` in `finally`. Approved UI pattern: `attemptWhilePending` catches, calls `onFailure()`, and does not rethrow. Approved store pattern: `PersistQueue` swallows a prior rejection so later writes still run — intentional chain recovery, not an empty catch.

### Use Unchecked Exceptions

TypeScript has no checked exceptions. Do not invent Result/Either wrappers that force every layer to unwrap like Java checked throws. Domain services throw; the UI catches once at `attemptWhilePending`.

### Provide Context with Exceptions

Exception messages name what failed and include identifying context when useful (`Category not found: ${id}`). Do not throw a bare `Error()` with no message, or throw literals (gated by `no-throw-literal`).

### Define Exception Classes in Terms of a Caller's Needs

v1 needs no custom exception class hierarchy. Callers at the UI boundary need one behavior — show a failure string — so plain `Error` plus catch in `attemptWhilePending` is enough. Do not catch in domain services just to rewrap unless a real boundary requires it (store wrapping stays under Boundaries / PB-97).

### Define the Normal Flow

Do not use throw/catch for expected user validation — that obscures the happy path. Parse helpers return a named outcome (empty / ok / invalid) so the caller branches without a try. Leave real exceptions for unexpected domain failures.

### Don't Return Null

Prefer throw or an explicit outcome over null-as-error. PB exceptions: `amountCents: null` means a sparse or cleared cell; store `getById` may return `undefined` for a miss; parse helpers may use `null` or an `empty` status for absent input when that is domain meaning. Invalid user input must not be a silent null that callers misread as delete.

### Don't Pass Null

Prefer omitting optionals or using `undefined` for “not provided” in new APIs. Accepted: pass `amountCents: null` into save paths where null is the sparse-cell signal — problem-domain null, not error null.

## Boundaries (Ch.8)

ESLint: `no-restricted-imports` — Dexie / `BudgetDatabase` banned from `src/app`, `src/data/domains`, and `src/data/lib`; `DocumentStore` / `createDocumentStore` / `DOCUMENT_STORE` banned from `src/app`. Folder map and harness seam: `.cursor/rules/document-store.mdc`. Full `features → domains → store` import direction is enabled under Systems (Ch.11 / PB-100).

### Using Third-Party Code

Dexie is broad; PB owns a narrow façade. Only `src/data/store` imports Dexie or `BudgetDatabase`. Features inject domain services (`SettingsService`, `CategoryService`, `BudgetCellService`) — never third-party persistence types. Domains inject `DOCUMENT_STORE`, not Dexie.

### Exploring and Learning Boundaries

Learn Dexie at the store edge. Store specs (`document-store.spec.ts` and related) exercise `insert` / `list` / soft-delete the way production does. Do not scatter raw Dexie calls through features to explore the API.

### Learning Tests Are Better Than Free

Store-level specs are the Dexie upgrade safety net. Feature specs seed data with `provideDocumentStoreHarness` (and may use `COLLECTIONS` / `StoreDocument` from `store/types`) — not by constructing `BudgetDatabase` or calling `createDocumentStore` directly.

### Using Code That Does Not Yet Exist

`DOCUMENT_STORE` and `DocumentStore` methods are the owned seam. Domains depend on that token; a future persistence swap adapts behind the same API. Features must not inject the token or import the store implementation.

### Clean Boundaries

Depend on what we control. Features → domain services (+ allowed `store/types` DTOs and `src/data/lib`); domains → `DOCUMENT_STORE`; Dexie only in `src/data/store`. Keep third-party references few so upgrades stay cheap.

## Classes (Ch.10)

ESLint: `max-classes-per-file` (1), `max-lines` (100, skip blank/comments), `@angular-eslint/sort-lifecycle-methods`, `@angular-eslint/component-max-inline-declarations`, `@angular-eslint/use-injectable-provided-in`. Specs/harnesses turn off `max-lines` / `max-classes-per-file` (dual standard with Ch.3). Already on via `angular.configs.tsRecommended` (do not re-own here): `prefer-inject`, `prefer-standalone`, `prefer-on-push-component-change-detection`. Angular file/class/selector conventions stay under PB-87. Full import-direction layering is enabled under Systems (Ch.11 / PB-100).

### Class Organization

Prefer the newspaper ordering from Formatting (Ch.5): public API and orchestration near the top; helpers step down. Enable lifecycle method sort via ESLint. Do not enable `@typescript-eslint/member-ordering` — it fights Angular `inject` / `input` / signal clusters and Ch.5 affinity judgment.

### Encapsulation

Keep collaborators private (`store`, injected services, private mutation helpers). Template-facing members stay `protected` (Angular requirement) — intentional, not a looseness smell. Prefer extracting a harness or helper over widening visibility for tests. Do not enable `explicit-member-accessibility`.

### Classes Should Be Small!

Gate size with `max-lines` and one class per file. Keep inline templates/styles tiny (`component-max-inline-declarations`); prefer `templateUrl` / `styleUrl`. Class names describe one responsibility in a short phrase without “and/or/if.” Weasel names (`Manager`, `Processor`, dumping-ground `Helper`) are a smell — prefer PB domain vocabulary from Meaningful Names (Ch.2).

### The Single Responsibility Principle

One reason to change per injectable or component. Domain services stay collection-scoped (`CategoryService`, `BudgetCellService`, `SettingsService`). Feature components orchestrate one UI concern; persistence stays in domains/store (Boundaries). If a class description needs “and,” extract a helper module or sibling component — do not grow god components. No SRP ESLint rule; judgment only.

### Cohesion

Fields and methods should hang together as one logical unit. Prefer extracting pure helpers to `helpers/` / `src/data/lib/` (Functions) over splitting a cohesive façade into artificial types. High method count alone is not a violation when every method serves the same reason to change; size is gated by `max-lines`. Exception-candidate: `DocumentStore` is a deliberate non-Angular façade (no `@Injectable`) with file-level helpers below the class — keep it under `max-lines` without forcing DI onto it.

### Organizing for Change

Prefer many small classes that isolate change. Do not pile unrelated feature concerns into one service for convenience. New `@Injectable`s declare `providedIn` (or an explicit provider at the owned boundary). Suffix/selector structure rules stay under PB-87.

### Isolating from Change

Depend on stable PB seams (`DOCUMENT_STORE`, domain services), not concrete third-party types — reinforces Boundaries. Full `features → domains → store` import direction is enabled under Systems (Ch.11 / PB-100).

## Systems (Ch.11)

ESLint: `no-restricted-imports` for dependency direction (builds on Boundaries / PB-97) — domains and lib must not import `src/app`; lib must not import domains; store must not import `src/app` or domains. Folder map and allowed seams: `.cursor/rules/document-store.mdc`. “Features” means the UI layer (`src/app` as a whole: screens, `shared/`, shell) — not a required `src/app/features/` folder (Angular structure stays under PB-87).

### Separate Concerns into Independently Developable Modules

Four zones: `src/app` (features / UI), `src/data/domains` (domain services + Zod DTOs), `src/data/lib` (pure helpers), `src/data/store` (Dexie + `DocumentStore`). Keep each zone independently developable; do not grow cross-layer convenience re-exports that invert the graph. Within `src/app`, screens may import `shared/`; `shared/` must not import screens.

### Separation of Construction and Use

Features **use** domain services via `inject()` — they never construct `DocumentStore` or Dexie (PB-97). Domains **use** `DOCUMENT_STORE`; construction lives in `createDocumentStore` and harness providers. Do not add a second Angular store façade over domains.

### Scaling Up / Keep the Architecture Modular

Enforce one-way dependencies: features → domains → store. Lib is a leaf under features and domains. Do not let store or domains reach upward into the UI.

### Proxies / AOP / AspectJ-Style Wiring

Java proxy and AspectJ material does not apply to this Angular/TS app. Angular DI plus the `DOCUMENT_STORE` token are the PB stand-in for wiring concerns at the edge — not new AOP tooling.

### Test-Drive the System Architecture

Architecture stays provable: store specs exercise real `DocumentStore` behavior; feature and domain specs use `provideDocumentStoreHarness` (allowed seam; production app still banned from the harness by PB-97). Vitest style stays under Unit Tests (Ch.9).

### Optimize Decision Making

The owned seam is `DOCUMENT_STORE` plus domain services so persistence tech can change behind the store without rewriting features. Dexie and concrete store bans stay with Boundaries (Ch.8); this chapter owns direction.

### Use Standards Wisely When They Add Demonstrable Value

Automate reverse/upward imports only — no heavyweight module-boundary plugin. Exception-candidates (allowed): features → `store/types` (`StoreDocument`, `COLLECTIONS`, …); features and domains → `src/data/lib`; specs/harnesses → harness + `store/types`.

### Systems Need Domain-Specific Languages

PB vocabulary (Meaningful Names, Ch.2) and domain services are the app’s DSL: features speak `CategoryService` / `BudgetCellService` / `SettingsService`, not Dexie.

## Emergence (Ch.12)

No new ESLint rules for this chapter. Kent Beck’s four rules of simple design are judgment plus overlap with earlier automate Tasks; duplication lint stays deferred to Smells (Ch.17 / PB-104).

### Runs All the Tests

A design that cannot be tested is not simple. Prefer seams that keep Vitest green: domain services, `provideDocumentStoreHarness` in specs, and pure helpers in `helpers/` / `src/data/lib`. Ticket finish bar stays `npm run lint` and `npm test` green. FIRST / AAA detail lives in `.cursor/rules/unit-tests.mdc` (Ch.9) — do not restate that matrix here.

### Contains No Duplication

Prefer one named extract over copy-paste. When two blocks share a concept, lift it once into `helpers/` or `src/data/lib` (Functions, Ch.3). Do not install duplication lint here — `eslint-plugin-sonarjs` and related smell gates belong to PB-104 so this chapter does not re-own earlier or later automate.

### Expresses the Intent of the Programmer

Intention-revealing names and small extracts beat comments that narrate what the next line does. Prefer PB vocabulary (`Category`, `amountCents`, `BudgetCell`, …). Naming, comment discipline, and extract style stay under Meaningful Names (Ch.2), Comments (Ch.4), and Functions (Ch.3). Refactor toward clarity once the tests are green.

### Minimizes the Number of Classes and Methods

Fewest elements that still express intent and pass tests — not a race to merge unrelated concerns. Do not invent pass-through wrappers, empty façades, or speculative types. Size gates already live under Functions (Ch.3) and Classes (Ch.10). Exception-candidate: do not collapse SRP injectables (`CategoryService` vs `BudgetCellService`) or Angular template/DI splits just to shrink the class count; Ch.10 SRP and PB-87 structure win when they conflict with “fewer files.”
